{
  "coreName": "xemu",
  "systemId": "XBOX",
  "systemNames": {
    "XBOX": "Xbox"
  },
  "author": "Matt Borgerson and the xemu contributors; QEMU is the work of Fabrice Bellard and the QEMU team",
  "url": "https://github.com/xemu-project/xemu",
  "romFile": "dvd",
  "deterministic": true,
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    512,
    16,
    416,
    256,
    1760
  ],
  "_memoryLayoutMiB_note": "sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap - the layout run-wbx.c proved. QEMU is enormous: glib wants a fat heap, the machine's 64-128MB of RAM lives in the mmap arena, and the COW overlays for the HDD and DVD grow there too. The 256 MB TCG translation cache is INVISIBLE memory (patch 0017): it is in no savestate, and StateLoaded flushes it.",
  "video": {
    "gpuStatesSurviveTheContext": true,
    "_comment_gpuStates": "The renderer rebuilds its GL objects when the calls land on a context that is not the one they came from (the bridge's GL_OP_CONTEXT_ID), so a state this core makes opens again in a later session - its greenzone survives a reload instead of being discarded.",
    "_comment": "buffer CAPACITY; the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height per frame. The machine's common mode is 640x480; the GL renderer can scan out up to 1080i, and internalResolution at 3x draws the common mode at 1920x1440, so the buffer takes that. A presented frame larger than the buffer (a 720p mode at 2x) falls back to the machine's own-size picture out of RAM. The vsync fraction is EXACTLY the driver's frame schedule: 60000/1001 (NTSC, what real Xbox video hardware scans out), walked as absolute deadlines of 50,050,000ns per three frames since the quantum is not a whole nanosecond - see VBLANK_3FRAMES_NS in the driver.",
    "width": 1920,
    "height": 1440,
    "virtualWidth": 640,
    "virtualHeight": 480,
    "vsyncNumerator": 60000,
    "vsyncDenominator": 1001,
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "the APU monitor's 48kHz stereo stream; ~800 pairs arrive per 60Hz frame and the accumulator holds 16384 for frames the frontend skips draining.",
    "rate": 48000,
    "samplesPerFrame": 16384,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "extensions": {
    ".iso": "XBOX",
    ".xiso": "XBOX"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "Xbox Controller",
    "_comment": "Four Duke pads, always plugged - the machine's USB topology is part of the machine, so the ports do not come and go with settings. The button list is in the core's packed-bit order (the xemu CONTROLLER_BUTTON enum) and the axes in SetAxis index order (LTrig, RTrig, LStick X/Y, RStick X/Y per port); reordering either breaks the wire format. The triggers are analog 0..32767; the face buttons the hardware reports as pressure are declared digital, which is how nearly every game reads them.",
